In this lesson we'll look at two simple rules that let you rearrange a calculation to make it easier in your head. First up is the commutative law, which just means you can swap the order of an addition or multiplication and still get the same answer. So three plus seventeen equals seventeen plus three, and four times twenty-five equals twenty-five times four. Quick heads-up though, subtraction and division don't work this way, so five minus three is definitely not three minus five. Next is the associative law, where you can regroup an addition or multiplication and the answer stays the same. For example, two plus eight in brackets, all plus thirty-five, equals two plus eight plus thirty-five in brackets. Same idea with multiplication. So why do these matter? Because they let you reorder and regroup so the numbers pair up nicely. Twenty-five plus seventeen plus seventy-five becomes twenty-five plus seventy-five plus seventeen, which is a hundred plus seventeen, giving you one hundred and seventeen. The same trick works in algebra too, letting you collect like terms easily. Now let's see it in action.